# 1️⃣ Recursive Binary Search

The logic is the same as iterative, but instead of looping, we call the function again with a smaller subarray.

### Code (C):

```c
#include <stdio.h>

int binary_search_recursive(int arr[], int left, int right, int target) {
    if (left > right) {
        return -1; // base case: not found
    }

    int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

    if (arr[mid] == target) {
        return mid; // found
    }
    else if (arr[mid] > target) {
        return binary_search_recursive(arr, left, mid - 1, target);
    }
    else {
        return binary_search_recursive(arr, mid + 1, right, target);
    }
}

int main() {
    int arr[] = {4, 5, 6, 7, 8, 9, 10};
    int n = sizeof(arr) / sizeof(arr[0]);
    int target = 9;

    int result = binary_search_recursive(arr, 0, n - 1, target);

    if (result != -1)
        printf("Element found at index %d\n", result);
    else
        printf("Element not found\n");

    return 0;
}
```

🔹 **Time Complexity**: `O(log n)`  
🔹 **Space Complexity**: `O(log n)` (because recursion stack adds up calls)  

> In industry, iterative is preferred because it avoids extra recursion overhead, but recursion helps you **think recursively** about divide & conquer.

---

# 2️⃣ Binary Search Variations

This is where students usually mess up because the simple `== target` isn’t enough.  
Let’s go through the **most asked variations**:

---

### a) First Occurrence of Element

If an element appears multiple times, **find the first index**.

🔹 Idea:  
- Even if `arr[mid] == target`, don’t stop immediately.  
- Check if it’s the **first** one by verifying `mid == 0 || arr[mid - 1] != target`.  
- If not, search on the **left side**.

```c
int first_occurrence(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = n - 1, ans = -1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            ans = mid;
            right = mid - 1;  // keep searching left
        }
        else if (arr[mid] > target) {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
        else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
```

---

### b) Last Occurrence of Element

Similar logic, but after finding target, keep searching right.

```c
int last_occurrence(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = n - 1, ans = -1;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] == target) {
            ans = mid;
            left = mid + 1;  // keep searching right
        }
        else if (arr[mid] > target) {
            right = mid - 1;
        }
        else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
```

---

### c) Lower Bound (first element ≥ target)

Think of `std::lower_bound()` in C++.  
Example: Array = `[2, 4, 6, 8]`, target = 5 → returns index `2` (element `6`).

```c
int lower_bound(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = n - 1, ans = n; // default = n (not found)

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] >= target) {
            ans = mid;
            right = mid - 1;
        } else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
```

---

### d) Upper Bound (first element > target)

Example: Array = `[2, 4, 6, 8]`, target = 6 → returns index `3` (element `8`).

```c
int upper_bound(int arr[], int n, int target) {
    int left = 0, right = n - 1, ans = n;

    while (left <= right) {
        int mid = left + (right - left) / 2;

        if (arr[mid] > target) {
            ans = mid;
            right = mid - 1;
        } else {
            left = mid + 1;
        }
    }
    return ans;
}
```

---

## 🔥 Summary

- **Basic binary search** → just finds an element.  
- **Recursive binary search** → teaches recursion, but iterative is faster in practice.  
- **Variations** (first/last occurrence, lower/upper bound) → extremely important in coding interviews.  

